DATA DECISIONS TRACKER

1. Household Pulse Survey
  A. Coding of Race
    - combined "other" and "multiracial" into just "other"
    - if respondent responded "yes" to Hispanic, they're hispanic
    - so, ended with 4 categories of race: NHW, NHB, NHO, HISP
  B. Coding of Sexual Orientation/Gender Identity
    - coded as trans/gnc if ANY of the following are true:
      1. responded "transgender" to genid_describe OR
      2. responded "nonbinary/other" to genid_describe OR
      3. responded "female" to genid_describe when genid_birth was "male" OR
      4. responded "male" to genid_describe when genid_birth was "female" OR
        - trans/gnc is missing if genid_describe is missing
    - coded as cis LGBQ if ALL of the following are true:
      1. trans_gnc is 0 (NOT missing) AND
      2. sexual_orientation is anything but "straight" (NOT missing)
        - cis_lgbq is missing if trans_gnc is missing or if sexual_orientation is missing
    - coded as queer if ANY of the following are true:
      1. trans_gnc is 1 OR
      2. cis_lgbq is 1 OR
      3. trans_gnc is missing, AND sexual_orientation is anything but "straight" (NOT missing)
        - queer is missing if sexual_orientation is missing and trans_gnc is not 1
        
2. Anti-Trans Laws Data
  A. ACLU Data
    - used archived data from 2018-2022, and published data from 2023 (urls available in laws_data.R)
    - for 2018-2022, only considered those laws under the umbrella category of "Anti-Trans Legislation"
    - for 2023, the laws weren't in umbrella categories, so I only considered laws with one of the following tags:
      1. public accomodations bans
      2. school facilities bans
      3. school sports bans
      4. healthcare restrictions
      5. drag bans
      6. barriers to accessing accurate ID
  B. LegiScan Data
    - looked up each tracked bill from the ACLU list compiled above
    - only recorded dates for bills that became law
    - if the URL implied by the original ACLU bill was not findable on LegiScan, made an effort to find it by Googling
    - in a few cases, the implied URL led to a bill that was not clearly anti-trans; in those cases, either...
      1. Googled the bill, attempted to find a bill of the same or close number that was anti-trans, went back to LegiScan with new bill
      2. If 1 was unsuccessful and bill did not pass, marked "dead" and moved on
        - No bills that passed were unsuccessful on 1
        
